   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 4.2. Strengthened capacity of enterprises to adopt new business models, technology and techniques to enhance productivity and sustainability</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">A network of trainers from different public and private organizations (National SME agency SMEPS, TVET departments, Sustainable development Foundation and several NGOs) expanded their service offer to train different population groups with varying needs, using the &#x201c;My first business&#x201d; entrepreneurship training for youth, and the &#x201c;I own a small business&#x201d; training for semi-literate persons.
In addition, a network of private sector representatives, in close collaboration with selected constituents (Chamber of Commerce) started rolling out the &#x201c;Business continuity planning and resilience&#x201d; training tool to help small businesses to sustain in the face of crises and become more resilient. 

Moreover, a network of 30 local trainers from private and public organizations and training centers has started offering the &#x201c;StartCoop&#x201d; and &#x201c;ThinkCoop&#x201d; training tools to promote cooperatives in the agricultural sector. With support from the European Union, the ILO organized a refresher training workshop on the updated training package &#x201c;I own small business&#x201d; for 39 selected trainers, as well as a refresher training on the &#x201c;My first business&#x201d; training package for 25 trainers from the national trainer network. The ILO also conducted a training of trainers&#x2019; for 52 new trainers from the network on ILO training package &#x201c;I own a small business&#x201d; aimed to support semiliterate beneficiaries. With ILO support, the trainer network trained 2,500 final beneficiaries on the two packages, and supported 1,200 rural women entrepreneurs with trainings and additional business development support. 
In addition, the ILO organized a training on &#x201c;Business Continuity Planning and Resilience&#x201d; for 15 partners from the private sector in close collaboration with the Chamber of Commerce, and subsequently supported the network in rolling out the training tool.

Moreover, the ILO conducted a needs assessment of cooperative enterprises and other similar producers groups to understand the situation and capacity of these groups in the selected value chains particularly. Following the assessment, ILO adapted the cooperatives training packages &#x201c;THINK COOPS, START COOPS and My COOPS&#x201d; to the specific context in Yemen and the specific needs of project beneficiaries. ILO then organized two training of trainers&#x2019; workshops to train 30 local trainers from various organizations on these training packages. With ILO support, these trainers trained over 80 cooperatives and rural producer groups on the different training packages.</narrative>
   </description>
